a(31,35,40,0.85)] dark:text-[rgba(184,194,205,0.9)]\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"This page is for developers building integrations against the bridge contracts and the \",\"$L43\",\". If you're an end user trying to figure out where your funds are, start with \",\"$L44\",\" and the \",\"$L45\",\". For protocol-level background, see \",\"$L46\",\" and \",\"$L47\",\".\"]}]}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"$L48\",null,{\"as\":\"h2\",\"id\":\"tracing-retryables-from-parent-chain-to-child-chain\",\"children\":\"Tracing retryables from parent chain to child chain\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"For the protocol-level model behind \",[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"title\":\"Retryable Ticket\",\"content\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"A parent-to-child cross-chain message initiated by a parent chain transaction sent to an Arbitrum chain for execution (e.g., a token deposit).\"}],\"children\":\"retryable tickets\"}],\", see \",\"$L4a\",\". If you only need to redeem a stuck retryable rather than trace it, use the \",\"$L4b\",\".\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"If you want to trace the \",[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"title\":\"Parent chain\",\"content\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"EVM compatible chain that acts as the settlement layer for one or more Arbitrum Chains (aka \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",null,{\"href\":\"/glossary#child-chain\",\"children\":\"Child chain\"}],\" ). For example, Ethereum is the parent chain of both Arbitrum One and Arbitrum Nova. Parent chain is synonymous with \\\"underlying chain.\\\"\"]}],\"children\":\"parent chain\"}],\" \",[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"title\":\"Transaction\",\"content\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"A user-initiated interaction with a Blockchain. Transactions are typically signed by users via wallets and are paid for via transaction fees.\"}],\"children\":\"transaction\"}],\" to the \",[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"title\":\"Child chain\",\"content\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"An Arbitrum Chain that settles to a \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",null,{\"href\":\"/glossary#parent-chain\",\"children\":\"Parent chain\"}],\". For example, Arbitrum One and Arbitrum Nova are child chains of Ethereum.\"]}],\"children\":\"child chain\"}],\" retryable ticket, the simplest way is to first create a provider using ethers via \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"providers.JsonRpcProvider(RPC)\"}],\". You should have one for both the parent and child chain. Then, using the parent chain provider, get the transaction via \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"parentProvider.getTransactionReceipt(Hash)\"}],\". Wrap the receipt in the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"ParentTransactionReceipt\"}],\" class, which allows you to call the class function \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"getParentToChildMessages(childProvider)\"}],\". This returns a parent-to-child message, in which one of the values is the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"retryableCreationId\"}],\" which is equivalent to the hash. You can then check if it has been submitted to the child chain by searching for that transaction either with the SDK (shown below) or a block explorer.\"]}],\"\\n\",\"$L4c\",\"\\n\",\"$L4d\",\"\\n\",\"$L4e\",\"\\n\",\"$L4f\",\"\\n\",\"$L50\",\"\\n\",\"$L51\",\"\\n\",\"$L52\",\"\\n\",\"$L53\",\"\\n\",\"$L54\",\"\\n\",\"$L55\",\"\\n\",\"$L56\",\"\\n\",\"$L57\",\"\\n\",\"$L58\",\"\\n\",\"$L59\",\"\\n\",\"$L5a\",\"\\n\",\"$L5b\",\"\\n\",\"$L5c\",\"\\n\",\"$L5d\",\"\\n\",\"$L5e\",\"\\n\",\"$L5f\",\"\\n\",\"$L60\",\"\\n\",\"$L61\",\"\\n\",\"$L62\",\"\\n\",\"$L63\",\"\\n\",\"$L64\",\"\\n\",\"$L65\",\"\\n\",\"$L66\",\"\\n\",\"$L67\",\"\\n\",\"$L68\",\"\\n\",\"$L69\",\"\\n\",\"$L6a\"]}]\n6c:{\"id\":\"408d49458f9248308470d3ee99ef3f34751766ee2f\",\"bound\":null}\n40:[\"$\",\"$L6b\",null,{\"onSendAction\":\"$h6c\"}]\n6d:I[107403,[\"/_next/static/immutable/chunks/14rxq3r5gs6l9.js\",\"/_next/static/immutable/chunks/0z6_p_zclhzeb.js\",\"/_next/static/immutable/chunks/2ckl54g9eb9wu.js\",\"/_ne